{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And rend:</b> Such was their custom when evil came upon them — to rend their garments, as in \"and Jacob rent his garments,\" and similarly in the words of David. And the rending of the heart is in the manner of \"circumcise the foreskin of your heart,\" and it is written: \"I will rend the covering of their heart.\" Now the meaning is to remove the heart's covering by rending it, for it prevents the heart from understanding the truth, and on account of this it is written: \"the prudent acts with knowledge.\"" "<b>And rend [as a noun/adjective]:</b> It is a descriptive noun. Were it not for the guttural letter, the pe would have received a dagesh for the nun to be assimilated."], :book :joel, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "The three scriptural quotations are Genesis 37:34, Deuteronomy 10:16 (or Jeremiah 4:4), and an unclear third reference; the phrase ואקרע סגור לבם does not correspond exactly to any known biblical verse and may reflect a variant reading or paraphrase.", 1 "The lemma ונחם appears to be a copyist's error for the form under discussion; Ibn Ezra's comment addresses the morphology of the nun in a word where guttural preventation of dagesh applies, likely וקרעו from the same verse."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Joel -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/yoel"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:06:34.839835933Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}